You need to send a two-line room booking to a volunteer and keep a
record of a missing attachment. You want one file that contains the
booking and another that contains only what went wrong. You don't
want a command's warning pasted into the message, and you definitely
don't want to erase the original booking while trying to make the
copy. A useful redirection makes all three outcomes predictable:
where the input comes from, where the ordinary output goes, and
where the diagnostics go.

\begin{objectives}
\item Connect the three standard channels to files without mixing their contents.
\item Explain truncation and appending, and avoid redirecting onto a source file.
\item Follow descriptor duplication from left to right.
\end{objectives}

\section{Build a lab before writing anything}
\label{sec:16-lab}
Use a fresh \cmd|sh| session. Make and enter a new private
directory with \cmd|mktemp -d|. The shell captures the path
in \texttt{lab}, preserves it with quotes, and stops proceeding
through the \cmd|&&| chain at the first failure.

\begin{terminal}
lab=$(mktemp -d) && cd "$lab" && pwd
\end{terminal}

Record the printed path. Stop if it doesn't identify a new
directory. All writing examples refer only to this directory.
\cmd|printf| prints each phrase followed by a newline. The
\cmd|>| changes where the command writes, from the terminal
to \texttt{booking.txt}; \cmd|cat| then reads it by name.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'Room 4' 'Thursday 18:00' > booking.txt
cat booking.txt
\end{terminal}

The term \emph{redirection} means the shell changes what a
descriptor is connected to before the command uses it. This is
shell syntax. In the example, \cmd|printf| gets a format and
two data arguments, not \cmd|>| or \cmd|booking.txt|. You can
use the same output syntax for completely different utilities
with no special cooperation from them. Where those utilities
send particular messages is still their decision.
\index{redirection}%

\section{Choose a new destination}
\label{sec:16-truncate}
\index{truncation}%
\index{append}%
With \cmd|>| the shell normally opens an existing ordinary
destination file and sets its length to zero as part of the
redirection. That's \emph{truncation}. It happens before the
command can read its input or tell you whether it has anything
to print. If the file doesn't exist, the shell normally creates
it, subject to the containing directory's permissions. To preserve
previous contents, make a new output file, or use \cmd|>>| when
the intended operation really is to add to an existing record.
Appending doesn't supply a separator or newline on its own;
the text being written must contain any required boundaries.

\begin{terminal}
cat booking.txt > handoff.txt
printf '%s\n' 'Bring key' >> handoff.txt
cat handoff.txt
\end{terminal}

You should see three lines. The third belongs to the handoff,
not to the booking, and reading \texttt{booking.txt} again
shows the original two lines. The following example uses an
explicitly disposable file: do not substitute a name you care
about. \cmd|false| writes nothing and returns a nonzero status.
Look at the file after it runs.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' throwaway > disposable.txt
false > disposable.txt
printf 'Status: %s\n' "$?"
wc -c < disposable.txt
\end{terminal}

\cmd|wc -c| counts bytes; \cmd|<| makes the file its standard
input. The count is zero despite the nonzero status. In
Figure~\ref{fig:16-open}, making the destination writable is a
precondition for running the command, not a reward the shell
grants it after success. If the destination cannot be opened
at all, the command won't run. If it can be opened and the
command later fails, you might have an empty or partial output
file. Its existence isn't proof of a successful run.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-16/open.tikz}
\caption{Opening the destination comes before running the command.}
\label{fig:16-open}
\VisualDescription{The shell opens or truncates the destination first. The command then runs. Its failure leaves the earlier file change in place.}
\end{figure}

\begin{warningbox}[Never use a source as its own destination]
Don't try to rewrite a source by giving it to a command and
redirecting that command's output back onto the same file. The
shell could truncate it before the program reads the first
byte. Two different names may also refer to the same file
through links (Chapter~\ref{ch:8}). Use a new destination in a
directory you control, check the result, and preserve a copy of
the source before replacing anything. An unverified recovery
tool is not a backup, and redirection has no general undo.
\end{warningbox}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.25in}Y}
\toprule
Shell syntax & Connection requested \\
\midrule
< file & Open file for reading on descriptor 0. \\
> file & Open file for writing on descriptor 1, normally truncating existing contents. \\
>> file & Open file for appending on descriptor 1, creating it if needed. \\
2> file & Open file for writing on descriptor 2; this also normally truncates. \\
2>> file & Open file for appending on descriptor 2. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

An unquoted descriptor number belongs immediately next to
the operator. In \cmd|2> notes.txt| the number identifies the
channel; with a space before \cmd|>| in \cmd|2 > notes.txt|,
the number can become a command argument instead. Whitespace
after \cmd|>| and before the filename is fine. Quote a
destination path that contains spaces just as you do other
paths. Use the portable form \cmd|2>| instead of learning a
shell-specific shortcut as if it worked everywhere.

\section{Keep data and diagnostics apart}
\label{sec:16-split}
\index{standard error!redirecting}%
An incoming booking is usable even when a second attachment
is missing, as long as the missing attachment doesn't disappear
from your record. \cmd|cat| emits the available file on standard
output; the failure to open the missing file goes to standard
error. Direct them to different new files:

\begin{terminal}
cat booking.txt missing.txt > included.txt 2> problem.txt
printf 'Status: %s\n' "$?"
cat included.txt
cat problem.txt
\end{terminal}

Expect a nonzero status, a two-line booking, and a diagnostic
whose wording depends on the implementation and locale.
Keeping the streams apart lets you forward the booking as
plain data while investigating the diagnostic. It doesn't
answer whether an incomplete bundle ought to be forwarded.
That depends on whether the missing attachment was essential.
If you plan to share diagnostics, read them first; a real
message may contain full paths or information meant only for
the local user.

\begin{terminal}
wc -l < booking.txt > count.txt
cat count.txt
\end{terminal}

This sets up two directions: descriptor 0 reads the booking,
while descriptor 1 sends the result to a separate file.
Expect two without a filename because the utility received no
file operand. A command that doesn't read standard input won't
use whatever you connected there, but the shell still attempts
to open any named input. Chapter~\ref{ch:15} explains why a
filename operand and standard input are different ways to
supply data.

\section{A duplicate follows the current connection}
\label{sec:16-order}
\index{file descriptor!duplication}%
Sometimes you want one combined report for a human reader.
\cmd|2>&1| tells the shell to make descriptor 2 refer to the
connection descriptor 1 refers to \emph{at that moment}. The
ampersand makes the \cmd|1| a descriptor number, not a filename.
This is not a permanent rule that stderr will always follow
future changes to stdout. Redirections are applied left to
right. In the first line below, the shell sends descriptor 1
to the file and then duplicates that connection. In the second
line, the shell duplicates the original descriptor 1 first.

\begin{terminal}
cat booking.txt missing.txt > combined.txt 2>&1
cat booking.txt missing.txt 2>&1 > data-only.txt
\end{terminal}

Both calls are expected to return nonzero. The first doesn't
put a diagnostic on your screen because both streams point to
\texttt{combined.txt}. The second leaves the diagnostic on
whatever descriptor 1 was connected to initially, normally
your screen, while the booking alone goes to
\texttt{data-only.txt}. Read the files to check membership;
order in a combined report isn't a universal transcript of
events. Programs may buffer one stream differently from the
other.

\begin{terminal}
cat combined.txt
cat data-only.txt
\end{terminal}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-16/order.tikz}
\caption{Copying a connection before or after moving it leads to different results.}
\label{fig:16-order}
\VisualDescription{With output redirected first, both numbered descriptors connect to the file. With the duplicate first, descriptor 2 retains the original terminal connection while descriptor 1 goes to the file.}
\end{figure}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.65in}Y}
\toprule
Order of redirections & Where the diagnostic goes \\
\midrule
> combined.txt 2>\&1 & The named file; descriptor 1 had already been connected there when it was duplicated. \\
2>\&1 > data-only.txt & The original descriptor 1 destination; the duplication occurred before descriptor 1 was redirected. \\
> included.txt 2> problem.txt & Its own named file, independent of descriptor 1. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-16/append.tikz}
\caption{Replacing a report and extending a report are different choices.}
\label{fig:16-append}
\VisualDescription{A file already contains lines A and B. Writing C with the replace operator leaves only C; writing C with the append operator leaves A, B, and C.}
\end{figure}

\begin{dialoguebox}{Copying a connection}
\Brian{I read \texttt{2>\&1} as ``send errors wherever the results go.''}
\Stephen{That is close: send them where the results go right now.}
\Brian{Why does ``right now'' matter? Surely the command hasn't started.}
\Stephen{It hasn't. The shell sets up each connection in the order you wrote.}
\Brian{If I move the results afterward, then I haven't moved the errors?}
\Stephen{Exactly. Moving one descriptor doesn't change the other.}
\Brian{Then I should write the file destination first when I want both in the file.}
\Stephen{Yes. Set the destination, then copy the connection.}
\DialogueConclusion{A duplicate shares the current destination.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Make the unwanted overwrite fail before it starts}
\label{sec:16-noclobber}
\index{noclobber}%
When trying an unfamiliar redirection, you can ask a
practice shell to reject an ordinary \cmd|>| aimed at an
existing regular file. \cmd|set -C| turns on this protection
in POSIX \cmd|sh|; \cmd|set +C| turns it off again. This
is a shell option, not a permanent attribute of the file.
Try it on a new disposable example instead of aiming a
test at your actual booking:

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'first copy' > protected.txt
set -C
printf '%s\n' 'second copy' > protected.txt
printf 'Status: %s\n' "$?"
set +C
cat protected.txt
\end{terminal}

Enter the lines at the interactive practice prompt. Expect
a diagnostic after the attempted overwrite, a nonzero status,
and \texttt{first copy} when you read the file. The failing
redirection isn't a failure of the \cmd|printf| format.
The shell never got as far as letting that invocation write.
If the first line fails because a prior run left
\texttt{protected.txt}, don't turn protection off just to
repeat that setup over whatever is there; use a new lab.

This protection, usually called \emph{noclobber}, is useful
but limited. It doesn't stop an append with \cmd|>>|,
and it doesn't control what a command does to a file it
opens itself. It is not a permission change or a history
of recoverable versions. A unique destination is a more
generally useful habit, especially if someone else may be
working in the same directory. Do not make a new result
by reusing a path you haven't checked or by assuming that
different-looking names are different files. Chapter~\ref{ch:8}
discusses links; the warning earlier in this chapter applies
whether the second name was given by you, a collaborator,
or a backup routine.

When several redirections are present, a mistake late in
the line doesn't roll back an earlier one. The following
uses two new targets, one of which cannot be opened because
its parent directory doesn't exist:

\begin{terminal}
cat booking.txt > opened-first.txt 2> absent-dir/problem.txt
printf 'Status: %s\n' "$?"
wc -c < opened-first.txt
\end{terminal}

The first redirection creates the new file. The second
fails before \cmd|cat| runs. Expect a nonzero status and
an empty \texttt{opened-first.txt}. If your shell's diagnostic
looks different from \cmd|cat|'s missing-file diagnostic,
there's a reason: the shell had to report that it couldn't
prepare the connection, before \cmd|cat| even tried to
open its operand. Inspect the line for a failed destination
before assuming that the named source is inaccessible.

\section{Know when the saved copy is ready}
When a redirected command works through a large input,
someone may see the output file appear early and mistake
it for a finished report. Wait for the command to finish
and check its status before passing that file on. Read a
sample and verify a count if the format gives you a
useful expected count. If the command was interrupted,
don't append a new attempt to the same output unless a
documented workflow tells you exactly where to resume.
Appending could repeat what it already wrote or attach
new text to an incomplete last line. Starting a fresh
output in that situation makes review simpler.

For the handoff at the start of the chapter, the safest
review is small: read the source and the new handoff,
verify the time and the added reminder, and keep the
diagnostic file out of the message. If the missing
attachment matters, ask for it explicitly rather than
quietly treating the two-line booking as a complete
bundle. A redirection chooses where facts are recorded;
it doesn't decide which facts are needed before you can
use the result.

\section{Change one connection at a time while diagnosing}
If a command has two redirections and doesn't run, test each
connection on its own before trying the whole line again.
Start with a read that cannot modify the booking. Use the
input path you set up and then a deliberately unavailable input:

\begin{terminal}
cat < booking.txt
cat < missing.txt > never-opened.txt
printf 'Status: %s\n' "$?"
ls
\end{terminal}

The first line reads from standard input rather than
receiving a filename operand and displays the booking.
On the second line the shell first tries to open the
nonexistent input; because that operation fails, it
doesn't get as far as creating \texttt{never-opened.txt}.
Check the nonzero status and notice that this name isn't
in the lab listing. Compare that with
\texttt{opened-first.txt}, which \emph{was} created when a
prior example opened the destination before attempting the
failing redirection. The shell stopped at the first failing
redirection in both cases; in only one case had it already
taken a step that left a file.

For a real diagnostic, read the whole command from left to
right and identify each destination before running it.
Some words that look like destinations aren't part of a
redirection at all. In \cmd|cat booking.txt|, the utility
opens the named operand. In \cmd|cat < booking.txt|, the
shell opens the file and \cmd|cat| reads the connection it
was given. When the first style fails to open a file, the
utility reports it; when the second fails, the shell does.
\cmd|cat| with no operands would otherwise read the
terminal, as Chapter~\ref{ch:15} demonstrated. Knowing
which layer was supposed to open which file helps explain
why the diagnostics may not use the same wording.

Consider the order when rerunning a report as well. If the
input path is uncertain, putting its redirection before
the output can keep a failed open from touching the output,
but it is not a guarantee that the source is readable to
the end or that the report will finish. Even a successful
open can be followed by a read or write error. Use a
separate destination for every attempt where losing the
last good report would matter. If a new version is empty
or partial, you can inspect it without confusing it with
the one you already verified.

Finally, don't aim stdout and stderr independently at the
same regular file to combine them. Something like
\cmd|> report.txt 2> report.txt| opens the pathname twice.
Besides the truncation issue, the two openings don't
share the position at which subsequent writes occur, so
parts of one stream can overwrite parts of the other.
The demonstrated \cmd|> combined.txt 2>&1| instead
duplicates an already opened destination. It can still mix
results with diagnostics, and it doesn't promise a
chronological transcript, but it doesn't independently
open the same file a second time. Choose between a combined
record and separate reports based on who will read the
result and whether descriptor 1 needs to carry clean data.

\section{Practice and cleanup}
\begin{exercise}{Extend a report}
Make a new \texttt{reminder.txt} containing the two booking
lines. Add \texttt{Check lights} as a third line without
touching the booking. Read both files.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd|cat booking.txt > reminder.txt| and then
\cmd|printf '%s\n' 'Check lights' >> reminder.txt|. The
reminder has three lines; the booking has its original two.
The final cleanup removes the optional reminder.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Capture a failed read}
Create separate \texttt{extra-data.txt} and \texttt{extra-error.txt}
while reading only the absent \texttt{missing.txt}. Find which
file is empty and check the status before reading either one.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd|cat missing.txt > extra-data.txt 2> extra-error.txt|,
then \cmd|printf '%s\n' "$?"|. Expect a nonzero status, zero
bytes in the data file, and a diagnostic in the error file.
The final cleanup includes both exercise outputs.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Predict the copied destination}
Read the booking followed by the missing file again. Use
\texttt{order.txt} for stdout but put the diagnostic where
stdout was originally. Predict the contents of the file.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd|cat booking.txt missing.txt 2>&1 > order.txt|.
The screen shows the diagnostic; \texttt{order.txt} contains
the two booking lines. The final cleanup includes this file.
\end{solution}

Check \cmd|pwd| against the recorded lab path. Only in that
directory, remove each named practice file with confirmation.
Omit names for optional exercises you didn't run. If you see
anything you want to keep, save it elsewhere before proceeding.
If \cmd|rmdir| fails, inspect what's left rather than switching
to recursive deletion. Leave your practice shell after cleanup.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i booking.txt handoff.txt disposable.txt included.txt problem.txt
rm -i count.txt combined.txt data-only.txt
rm -i protected.txt opened-first.txt
rm -i reminder.txt extra-data.txt extra-error.txt order.txt
cd ..
rmdir "$lab"
\end{terminal}

\begin{takeaways}
\item The shell opens the destination before the command can run.
\item Append only to a file that is meant to grow; use a separate fresh destination for transformations.
\item Read redirections from left to right, as changes to numbered connections.
\end{takeaways}

